Several generic string/sequence primitives are very slow in the interpreter,
enough to dominate real workloads (APROPOS, large-system LOAD, sorting).

Measured (v1.4.34, ~3200-symbol image):
- CHAR (string accessor): ~15 us per call.
- STRING< (generic, keyword-arg version at prelude ~7171): ~5 ms per call.
- SEARCH / MISMATCH (generic): ~14 ms per call (substring of a short pattern in
  a ~15-char target).
- SORT (list merge sort): ~3.8 s for 3200 integers with (function <); the merge
  sort is O(n log n) but the per-op cons/funcall overhead is huge; a large SORT
  with a STRING< comparator times out (minutes).

These made APROPOS take ~28 s (worked around in v1.4.35 with a %STRING-CONTAINS-CI
kernel primitive and %STRING-COMPARE-based sort — see slow-apropos.issue), but the
underlying primitives are still slow for everything else.

Directions:
- Fast paths for SIMPLE-STRING in CHAR/SCHAR/STRING</STRING=/etc. (kernel byte
  access, skip the generic dispatch + character-object boxing).
- A less allocation-heavy SORT (or a kernel sort primitive for the common
  list/vector-of-comparable case).
- Consider why the generic SEARCH/MISMATCH constant is so high (keyword parsing,
  per-element funcall of TEST/KEY, ELT dispatch).

Note: two STRING< definitions exist (prelude ~1545 using %STRING-COMPARE, and
~7171 the generic one that overrides it); the fast one is shadowed.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PARTIALLY RESOLVED in v1.4.36 — string comparison, SEARCH and MISMATCH.

Root cause confirmed: the cost is NOT in any single primitive but in the
interpreter's per-funcall overhead (~150 us).  CHAR is a thin wrapper over
char_ref; it is slow only because *calling* it from interpreted Lisp is slow.
So the fix is to move whole loops into C, exactly as v1.4.35 did for APROPOS —
not to speed up individual accessors.  bocl strings are octet arrays with one
octet per character (char_ref indexes bytes) and case folding is ASCII-only
(UPPER-CASE-P/LOWER-CASE-P), so a byte-wise C scan reproduces the Lisp semantics
exactly.

New kernel primitives (sources/bocl.c):
- %STRING-CMP2 (a b ci start1 end1 start2 end2) -> (values mismatch-index rel)
  the byte-wise bounded, optionally case-folded comparison.  %STRING-COMPARE,
  %STRING-COMPARE-BOUNDED and %STRING-CMP now all delegate to it, so every
  STRING</STRING>/STRING=/... and STRING-LESSP/... family and any SORT with a
  string comparator is fast.  The shadowed/duplicate Lisp CHAR-loop bodies were
  removed.
- %STRING-SEARCH (pattern target ci start2 end2) -> index | NIL, wired into
  SEARCH as a fast path for the common (string pattern, string target, default
  char test, no KEY, whole pattern, not FROM-END) case.  MISMATCH got an
  analogous fast path built on %STRING-CMP2.

Measured (~3200-symbol image), before -> after:
- STRING<   7217 us -> 475 us  (15x)
- STRING=   3455 us -> 749 us  (4.6x)
- SEARCH   33529 us -> 175 us  (192x)
- MISMATCH 15212 us -> 376 us  (40x)
A SORT with a STRING< comparator is ~15x cheaper per comparison as a result.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
STILL OPEN — SORT.

A Lisp-level SORT cannot be made fast in this interpreter: at ~150 us/op an
O(n log n) sort of a few hundred elements costs seconds regardless of algorithm.
An array-based bottom-up merge sort was tried and REJECTED — it measured ~29 s
for 3000 integers vs ~5.5 s for the existing list merge sort, because bocl's
AREF/MAKE-ARRAY are far costlier than CONS car/cdr.  The list merge sort
(%merge/%list-split/*%old-sort*) is retained as the best Lisp-level option.

The real fix is a kernel SORT primitive.  Design (deferred — it calls back into
Lisp per comparison, so it must be GC-safe; review before merging):
  1. Pin the input list; count N; copy the element Refs into a malloc'd C array.
     The elements stay reachable from the (unmodified) pinned input list, so the
     non-moving GC will not collect them during comparator callbacks.
  2. Bottom-up merge (or heap) sort the C array, comparing via kernel_apply(less,
     args, &r) with args pinned in an open live pool across the call — follow the
     rooting discipline in sources/sequences.c map_walk() (build args, add to
     live pool, keep pool open across kernel_apply; heap corruption otherwise).
  3. Write the sorted order back by RPLACA-ing the input list's conses in order
     (destructive; no allocation, so no GC during write-back).
Expected ~3-4x for integer/symbol sorts; string sorts are already comparator-
bound and covered by %STRING-CMP2 above.
